# Final Project for CISC 106

# UD Bus Tracker

Live University of Delaware bus wait times, and a planner that tells you
whether to walk or take the bus.

## Table of Contents
- [Description](#description)
- [Installation](#installation)
- [Usage](#usage)
- [Pages](#pages)
- [Data](#data)

## Description
The goal of this project is to determine whether it is better to walk to a
class/destination or to take the bus at the University of Delaware campus.
The site shows the 5 stops with the shortest and longest waits, which routes
are not running, and plans a trip between two campus places.

## Installation
```bash
pip install -r src/requirements.txt
```
The only package needed is drafter 2.1.3; everything else is the Python
standard library. Drafter runs the site inside the browser (Pyodide), so
`requests` and other socket-based packages would not work there.

## Usage
```bash
cd scripts
python main.py
```
Drafter opens the site at http://localhost:8000. The 69 unit tests run every
time the file loads; their results are shown under the page.

Drafter writes errors to `scripts/drafter-debug.log` (git-ignored). With
drafter 2.1.3 it always logs "Unhandled configuration update event" for the
`set_site_information` fields; that is a drafter issue and harmless.

## Pages
- **Home**: data status and how many routes are running.
- **Fastest stops / Slowest stops**: top 5 stops by wait for the next bus.
- **Route status**: running and not-running routes.
- **Plan a trip**: short how-to, and a map with a pin for every choice in
  the two lists (campus places and every bus stop, with matching names).
- **Trip result**: whether to walk or take the bus, numbered directions
  (walk to the stop, wait, ride, get off, walk), and a map showing the bus
  route in its color plus dashed walking lines.
- **Refresh**: reloads live data.

## Data
Bus data comes from the public ETA SPOT JSON service used by UD's bus app
(`https://udshuttle.etaspot.net/service.php`). The site loads it at start and
reloads it whenever a page is opened with data older than 60 seconds, since
arrival times count down from the moment they were loaded. Walking times use
straight-line distance at 5 km/h, so real walks are a bit longer. Campus
place coordinates are approximate.

Route shapes come from the same service as Google "encoded polylines".
Walking lines on the map are straight, matching how walk times are worked
out. The trip map uses Leaflet 1.9.4 (from unpkg) and OpenStreetMap tiles,
so it needs an internet connection.
